-- title:
--   Coordinate ring of the affine Drinfeld curve is Dedekind
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime number and let $k$ be a field which is an algebra over `GaloisField q 2`, the field with $q^2$ elements, and which is algebraically closed. Consider the quotient $$\mathrm{CoordRing}\;q\;k \;=\; k[X_0,X_1]\,/\,\bigl(\,\mathrm{drinfeldPoly}\,q\,k - 1\,\bigr),$$ that is, the quotient of the polynomial ring `MvPolynomial (Fin 2) k` in two variables by the ideal generated by the single element `drinfeldPoly q k - 1`, where `drinfeldPoly q k` is the two-variable Drinfeld polynomial over $k$. Assume in addition that this quotient ring is an integral domain. The assertion is then that `CoordRing q k` is a Dedekind domain in the sense of Mathlib's `IsDedekindDomain`: it is a noetherian integral domain of Krull dimension at most one (every nonzero prime ideal is maximal) which is integrally closed in its field of fractions. Thus the hypothesis that the quotient is a domain is imposed rather than proved here, and the content added is noetherianity, dimension at most one and integral closedness.
--
--   This is the algebraic counterpart of the smoothness of the affine Drinfeld curve: its affine coordinate ring is the Dedekind ring whose localisations at maximal ideals are the discrete valuation rings attached to the finite places of the associated function field. It is used in the analysis of places of the Drinfeld curve and in the study of the blow-up charts of the auxiliary level-one modular curve construction.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_DrinfeldCurve_isDedekindDomain_coordRing.lean

